{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01F1jM8WqkbZMrJmHhrQXyFL","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Fix Prompt for Failing Test /playwright/playwright:<startup>\n2\t\n3\t## Target\n4\tApply the change(s) under `services/*` only. The test at\n5\t`tests/integration/frontend-home.spec.ts` is the contract; do NOT modify it,\n6\tand do NOT modify `tests/playwright.config.ts`, `docker-compose.yaml`, `docs/`,\n7\tor `true-bdd/`.\n8\t\n9\t## Root Cause\n10\tThe test does:\n11\t\n12\t```ts\n13\ttest('home page greets with Hello world', async ({ page }) => {\n14\t  await page.goto('/');\n15\t  await expect(page.locator('h1')).toHaveText('Hello world');\n16\t});\n17\t```\n18\t\n19\t`tests/playwright.config.ts` boots the app via its `webServer` entry:\n20\t`docker compose up --build frontend` with `cwd: '..'`, waiting on\n21\t`http://127.0.0.1:${FRONTEND_PORT}`. `docker-compose.yaml` builds that service\n22\tfrom `context: ./services/frontend` and maps `${FRONTEND_PORT}:3000`, so the app\n23\tmust listen on port **3000 inside the container** and bind `0.0.0.0`.\n24\t\n25\tBut `services/frontend/` does not exist at all (there is no `services/`\n26\tdirectory in the repo). The compose build therefore fails, no server ever comes\n27\tup, Playwright's `webServer` wait times out and the test fails.\n28\t\n29\tThe fix is to create the minimal Next.js (App Router) frontend service that\n30\t`docs/architecture/architecture.yaml` declares (`services/frontend`,\n31\ttypescript + next.js + react, quality gate config at\n32\t`services/frontend/tsconfig.json`) and that renders an `<h1>` whose text is\n33\texactly `Hello world` at `/`.\n34\t\n35\t## Required Changes\n36\t\n37\tAll files below are NEW — use the **Write** tool for each one. Create them\n38\texactly at these paths with exactly this content.\n39\t\n40\t### Change #1: Service manifest\n41\t**File:** `services/frontend/package.json`\n42\t**Action:** Write (new file).\n43\t**Content:**\n44\t```json\n45\t{\n46\t  \"name\": \"frontend\",\n47\t  \"version\": \"0.1.0\",\n48\t  \"private\": true,\n49\t  \"scripts\": {\n50\t    \"dev\": \"next dev -H 0.0.0.0 -p 3000\",\n51\t    \"build\": \"next build\",\n52\t    \"start\": \"next start -H 0.0.0.0 -p 3000\"\n53\t  },\n54\t  \"dependencies\": {\n55\t    \"next\": \"15.1.6\",\n56\t    \"react\": \"19.0.0\",\n57\t    \"react-dom\": \"19.0.0\"\n58\t  },\n59\t  \"devDependencies\": {\n60\t    \"@types/node\": \"22.10.7\",\n61\t    \"@types/react\": \"19.0.7\",\n62\t    \"@types/react-dom\": \"19.0.3\",\n63\t    \"typescript\": \"5.7.3\"\n64\t  }\n65\t}\n66\t```\n67\tNotes for the applier: the `start`/`dev` scripts MUST keep `-H 0.0.0.0 -p 3000`\n68\t— compose publishes the host port onto container port 3000, and a loopback-only\n69\tbind is unreachable from the host, which is what Playwright probes.\n70\t\n71\t### Change #2: Next.js config\n72\t**File:** `services/frontend/next.config.mjs`\n73\t**Action:** Write (new file).\n74\t**Content:**\n75\t```js\n76\t/** @type {import('next').NextConfig} */\n77\tconst nextConfig = {\n78\t  reactStrictMode: true,\n79\t};\n80\t\n81\texport default nextConfig;\n82\t```\n83\t\n84\t### Change #3: TypeScript config (declared by the architecture quality gate)\n85\t**File:** `services/frontend/tsconfig.json`\n86\t**Action:** Write (new file).\n87\t**Content:**\n88\t```json\n89\t{\n90\t  \"compilerOptions\": {\n91\t    \"target\": \"ES2022\",\n92\t    \"lib\": [\"dom\", \"dom.iterable\", \"ES2022\"],\n93\t    \"allowJs\": false,\n94\t    \"skipLibCheck\": true,\n95\t    \"strict\": true,\n96\t    \"noEmit\": true,\n97\t    \"esModuleInterop\": true,\n98\t    \"module\": \"esnext\",\n99\t    \"moduleResolution\": \"bundler\",\n100\t    \"resolveJsonModule\": true,\n101\t    \"isolatedModules\": true,\n102\t    \"jsx\": \"preserve\",\n103\t    \"incremental\": true,\n104\t    \"plugins\": [{ \"name\": \"next\" }],\n105\t    \"paths\": {\n106\t      \"@/*\": [\"./*\"]\n107\t    }\n108\t  }